    "body": "Great on any Friday: Noco Boost X GBX45 - Portable Jump Box & Power Bank\nAll-New Bentley Flying Spur Goes British Racing Green With Round Headlights in Fresh Renderings\n7 photos\nPhoto: kelsonik / Instagram\nThe ultra-luxury British automaker Bentley is focused solely on its first all-electric nameplate, Torcal, a crossover SUV packing up to 876 hp on the dedicated PPE architecture shared with the recent Porsche Cayenne Electric.\nThe latest model was launched not long ago, in September 2026, sharing the Premium Platform Electric (PPE) with the mighty Porsche, but it has already received a special commission, dubbed Bentley x FRGMT Torcal , which is a fashion-inspired collaboration in 20 units with Hiroshi Fujiwara, “a pioneer of Tokyo’s Ura-Harajuku streetwear movement.”\nOf course, not everyone around the world needs or wants a zero-emission Bentley. The company still offers the ICE and hybrid Bentayga, Continental GT, and Flying Spur models, plus the limited Supersports, thus offering ample choice. But that doesn’t mean the new EV model, which introduces a fresh design language for the automaker, won’t influence the future of the other models in the lineup.\nAt least that’s the opinion across the rumor mill and the parallel universes of vehicular CGI where the members of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators usually dwell around. For example, Nikita Chuicko, the virtual artist better known as \" kelsonik \" on social media, thinks that now is the right CGI time to work on a new Bentley – all over again.\nInitially, the pixel master created an unofficial, hypothetical design project focused on the potential all-new fourth-gen (modern) Bentley Flying Spur or a massive refresh of the current third generation for the good folks over at Kolesa . The new sedan was heavily influenced by the styling of the Torcal, both at the front and around the back.\nAlas, that didn’t bode well for the CGI expert, who had other plans for Bentley – he returned for a comprehensive digital transformation of the ultra-luxury sedan, this time around in a vibrant crimson hue and only keeping the Torcal-influenced styling around the rear, where it’s less damaging. At the front, on the other hand, the author actually went back in time, and the new Flying Spur has returned to classic double round LED headlights, this time with LED daytime halo rings and the new style of grille as on the Torcal.\nBut wait, there was more – together with Kolesa, the virtual artist also thought about a refreshed or all-new Bentley Continental GT, and this time around the news outlet also thought the double round headlight idea was better than the one they had initially with the Flying Spur. And it was also dressed in another vibrant hue – this time a metallic orange shade .\nNope, we’re not done yet. Just recently, the pixel master has again refocused their attention on the Bentley Flying Spur – now dressed in classic British Racing Green attire and taken out of the studio against a rainy British backdrop. There’s even a comparison with the Rolls-Royce Ghost, too! View this post on Instagram A post shared by CAR DESIGN | RENDERINGS (@kelsonik)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBentley Flying Spur Bentley Flying Spur Rolls-Royce Ghost ultra-luxury sedan CGI new generation rendering kelsonik\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1993 Aston Martin Virage Vacances Was Born a Coupe, It Turned Into a Seven-Seat Shooting Brake",
